  "publishedAt": null,
  "contentHtml": "<figure><a href=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/%24s_!QfyU!,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F5df05998-c0b9-4faf-89fb-da38641e6acd_1900x1330.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">\n<img src=\"https://substackcdn.com/image/fetch/%24s_!QfyU!,w_1456,c_limit,f_auto,q_auto:good,fl_progressive:steep/https%3A%2F%2Fsubstack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fpublic%2Fimages%2F5df05998-c0b9-4faf-89fb-da38641e6acd_1900x1330.png\" width=\"1456\" height=\"1019\" data-size=\"normal\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\">\n\n\n</a><figcaption><strong>Quarterback Caleb Williams of the Chicago Bears in a preseason game between the Chicago Bears and the Cincinnati Bengals on August 22, 2026 in Cincinnati, OH. </strong>(Jeff Moreland/Icon Sportswire via Getty Images)</figcaption></figure>\n<p>In many ways, the story of the 2026 NFL season is how <em>weird </em>the <a href=\"https://www.pro-football-reference.com/years/2025/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">2025 season</a> was — and what will carry over from that, versus regressing away.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/subscribe?\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><span>Subscribe now</span></a></p>\n<p>In writing about some of the teams I find most interesting ahead of this week’s ‘26 season kickoff, I’ve already looked at <a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/p/the-chiefs-are-going-to-be-nfl-main\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">how the Chiefs should benefit</a> from their historically-awful luck swinging back toward normal (especially if we mean <em>their </em>normal <a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/p/football-bytes-can-the-chiefs-keep?utm_source=publication-search\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">from before</a>), and <a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/p/why-regression-to-the-mean-may-be\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">how the Cowboys could improve almost by default</a> because bad defenses regress positively more than good offenses do negatively. Immediately after the Super Bowl, I even wrote about how <a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/p/the-seahawks-next-and-harder-task\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">hard it will be for the champion Seahawks to avoid being a one-off</a>.</p>\n<p>But the 2026 Chicago Bears present maybe the most interesting case of all — particularly <a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/p/the-bears-are-stumbling-after-a-playoff?utm_source=publication-search\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">when we consider their history</a>. </p>\n<p>\n          <a href=\"https://neilpaine.substack.com/p/the-bears-are-coming-off-a-playoff\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">\n              Read more\n          </a>\n      </p>",
